{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261112T170952Z-c9ab731c", "ts": "2026-11-12T17:16:28Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "feature/review-round-cost", "build": "c599b926c4b8"}, "body": {"base_commit": "c77a00b0449be8d45ec3046b0fe610bff63d2650", "base_tree": "3b4f6cc394fc9c6f6d6ed712e1da05630ad67971", "head_tree": "e6d16f1df9e878159feae430f2ce38e0c2943989", "head_commit": "c7792de1c13a45393ec34810e4dfc5ac03c3c201", "dispatch_commit": "c7792de1c13a45393ec34810e4dfc5ac03c3c201", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/issues/847-design.md", "plugin/agents/critic-reviewer.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/dispositions.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/stats.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/coordinator.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "tests/test_carried_blocking_findings.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_critic_reviewer_agent.py", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_review_stats.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_test_run_facts.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-review-round-cost.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/review-round-cost.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "documentation/issues/847-design.md", "plugin/agents/critic-reviewer.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/dispositions.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/stats.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/coordinator.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "tests/test_carried_blocking_findings.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_critic_reviewer_agent.py", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_review_stats.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_test_run_facts.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-review-round-cost.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/review-round-cost.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "validate-partial reads the review from the caller's process, not from the partial it is handed, so a reviewer told it is 'done only on exit 0' never finishes when its process resolves another tree", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: `validate_partial_cmd` finds the manifest through `gitstate.get_prawduct_dir(project_dir)`, and `project_dir` comes from `get_project_dir()`, meaning the cwd's worktree top or CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR. It does not come from the absolute partial path the reviewer passes. Where that resolves a different checkout, the verb exits 1 every time. The failure is 'no readable review manifest', or 'is not a partial of review <other id> - write it at the path your manifest's rendezvous names', and nothing in the file can fix either one. critic-reviewer.md itself names this case: a worktree session whose subagent anchors to the primary checkout. Its new write step says 'on exit 1, fix the file and run it again. You are done only on exit 0.' A remedy the reviewer cannot satisfy means a loop, or a stray partial written at a path the wrong manifest names. This is the authoring rule 'A REMEDY named in a checklist must be tested against the predicate that produced the condition'. Every `TestValidatePartialVerb` case runs with cwd=repo, so no test sees this ('When every test INJECTS a dependency, green says nothing about how production OBTAINS it'). Fix: when the path is absolute and sits at `<dir>/.prawduct/.critic-partials/<file>`, take the prawduct dir from the path itself, or fall back to it when the cwd-resolved manifest does not own the path. Also mark refusals that are not about the file's content (no manifest, no roster partial) as 'report and stop', not 'fix the file'. Add a test that runs the verb from a sibling worktree of the same repo. To bound the class, check every reviewer-run verb whose instruction keys on its exit code: today that is this one only, since test-status and verify-coverage are reports.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/agents/critic-reviewer.md", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "An inherited failure already rated BLOCKING before the base run was recorded is never re-evaluated, yet the refusal line and docstring imply that recording the base run spares the verify round", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: `_waive_inherited_failures` considers only the findings of the review being consolidated (`review_id`), and only at that consolidation. A finding raised when no base-tree run existed is refused with 'no base-tree run to match: no whole-suite test run is recorded on tree X'. The docstring says that line exists 'so a builder who recorded a base run learns what it failed to prove rather than re-recording blindly or paying the verify round'. The builder's natural next step is to record the base run. Nothing then re-reads that finding: no later consolidation waives another review's finding, and the data-model rule now limits the waiver to 'a BLOCKING finding of the review whose consolidation writes it'. So the already-raised blocker clears only through the verify-resolutions round #900 set out to remove, or through a fresh review whose interval bypasses it. That is the order a builder meets most often: review first, then discover the base was red. Either (a) make the no-run refusal (and the change record's consumer note) say that this finding now clears through verify-resolutions, and that a base run recorded now serves only later reviews' findings; or (b) take to the owner whether a later consolidation may waive an unresolved test-failure blocker on its own chain from the same base run. That would amend the data-model constraint, so it is not a builder's call (gates rule: 'A message naming the caller's NEXT step must ask the gate that will judge that step').", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "validate-partial gives exit 1 both for a bad file and for 'nothing to check against', and the reviewer is told to fix the file on any exit 1 and stop only on exit 0", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the verb takes its review from process state (the cwd-resolved project's manifest) instead of from the path it is handed, and returns one exit code for two different conditions, while critic-reviewer.md maps every exit 1 to 'fix the file and run it again. You are done only on exit 0.' `critic_consolidate.validate_partial_cmd` exits 1 for (a) a defective partial and (b) no readable manifest, an invalid manifest, or 'not a partial of review X'. Three ordinary situations produce (b) for a sound partial, and in each one the instructed loop cannot end. First: reviewers run concurrently, and the new step widens the gap between a reviewer's Write and its stop. A sibling's SubagentStop can consolidate the complete roster inside that gap, and `remove_partials` then deletes the manifest. The late reviewer gets 'no readable review manifest' and is told to rewrite a file in a directory that consolidation just removed, which recreates debris. Second: the agent's own step 2 has the reviewer write a `dispatch-mismatch` partial when the manifest is unreadable or carries another review's id, and validate-partial refuses that partial by construction. This contradicts the 'then stop' that step 2 gives (Framework Check 8/9, cross-section consistency). Third: a subagent whose cwd resolves to a different checkout than the dispatched project (the case the agent file itself calls classic) reads the wrong manifest. The docstring's claim, 'resolved against the project directory, not the caller's cwd: a subagent's cwd is not the project', does not hold, because `get_project_dir` derives that project from the cwd. The authoring rule 'A REMEDY named in a checklist must be tested against the predicate that produced the condition' applies here. Fix by construction: (1) derive the review from the absolute partial path's own `.prawduct` dir (partial_path is `<prawduct>/.critic-partials/<role>.<id>.json`) instead of the cwd; (2) give 'cannot check' its own exit code, distinct from 'this file fails', and state in api-contract.md and critic-reviewer.md that only the file-failure code means fix-and-rerun, and that the other code means stop, because the review has either consolidated or is not yours to check. Pin it with a test run from a cwd other than the partial's project. The current relative-path test runs with cwd equal to the repo, so it cannot tell the two apart.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/agents/critic-reviewer.md", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The 'why it was not waived' line dies on the coordinator path: consolidate runs inside SubagentStop, which always exits 0, and the reason is persisted nowhere", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the refusal reason exists only as a stderr print in `_waive_inherited_failures`, and stderr from a hook that exits 0 is never shown to the model. On a `final`/`cumulative` coordinator roster (every boundary review, this one included), `critic-consolidate` runs from `cmd_subagent_stop`, which always returns 0, so the `refuse()` lines (`critic-consolidate: Rn names failing tests but was not waived as inherited \u2014 <reason>`) reach no reader. Consolidation succeeded, so the Stop backstop never re-runs it, and partials are removed, so a manual re-run is a no-op. `.critic-findings.json` marks only the findings that were waived. A builder who recorded a base run sees an ordinary BLOCKING finding with no reason anywhere. They then re-record blindly or pay the verify-resolutions round, which is the outcome the docstring says the line exists to prevent. The change record's consumer note ships the promise 'When a waiver does not fire, consolidation prints one line saying why', and that is true only on the single-pass path. Every refusal test calls `critic-consolidate` directly and reads `done.stderr` (tests/test_critic_consolidate.py ~4848, ~5002), so the suite is green on the path where the line is visible and silent about the one where it is not. Core rule 'A channel that is produced and never consumed is a DEFECT, not an inefficiency' applies. Remedy: carry the reason on a surface the builder reads after a dispatched review. Either derive it into the cache entry/NEXT-ACTION at render time, or record it where the session briefing or Stop hook surfaces it. Pin it with a test that drives consolidation through `trigger=\"subagent-stop\"` (or `prawduct-hook subagent-stop`) and asserts the reason reaches that surface. Bound the class by grepping `consolidate`'s success-path `print(..., file=sys.stderr)` diagnostics for others that die the same way.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "rule-unenforced: a telemetry report's key-set change bumps its schema_version and is documented \u2014 `waived_inherited` joins `by_outcome` and `stats` findings with neither", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the api-contract's Versioning rule ('Telemetry report carries its own `schema_version`, bumped on any change to its key set (`plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md`, pinned by `tests/test_review_stats.py`)') is pinned only for TOP-LEVEL keys (`TestJsonContractMatchesTheDoc`), so a nested key set can change without any check noticing. Here `stats._OUTCOME_OF_STATE` gains `waived_inherited`. That changes the fixed key set of `review-stats --json` `by_outcome` (telemetry.py builds it from `stats.OUTCOMES`) and of `stats --json` per-severity findings, a contract surface the cross-project aggregator consumes. Yet `telemetry.REPORT_SCHEMA_VERSION` (11) and `stats.REPORT_SCHEMA_VERSION` (3) do not move. `plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md`'s two outcome lists (~line 304, `by_outcome`: 'fixed, fixed_unreviewed, filed, accepted, waived, undispositioned, other'; ~line 459, the stats outcomes) omit it. Its acted-on paragraph says undispositioned is excluded and does not say this outcome is too. The plan's api-contract disposition ('additive-first evolution \u2192 conforms; one new read-only verb, one optional finding field, and two additive fact fields') does not count this key, so nothing shows downstream consumer impact was weighed. Consequence: an aggregator comparing reports across plugin versions cannot tell 'zero inherited waivers' from 'writer predates the outcome', which is the exact ambiguity the bump rule exists to remove. A reader of the doc cannot find what the new key means. Remedy: bump both schema versions with a 'Schema N added' line and add the outcome to both doc lists, or record why a nested outcome key is not a key-set change. Mechanising it takes one test asserting every `stats.OUTCOMES` name appears in the doc's `by_outcome` list and stats section. Pair it with a pinned outcome tuple whose change requires a schema bump.", "files": ["plugin/lib/stats.py"], "fid": "R-5"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 5, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Sweep survivors of 'only verify-resolutions records resolutions'", "recommendation": "Chunk 02's sweep made gates.py:1833 relational ('for an earlier review's findings') but left the same claim in four places. The first is gates.py:1796, in the same file. The others are critic_consolidate.py:985 (comment), critic_consolidate.py:2855 (the user-facing frontier-blocked clause, 'only `verify-resolutions` records resolutions') and critic_mode.py:1804 (docstring). Each is still true of what it describes, an earlier review's blocker, so nobody acts wrongly. If touched, use the same relational form; no edit is owed.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Readers of the findings cache that count severity == blocking do not read the new `waived` marker", "recommendation": "briefing.py (~1785) renders every cache finding with severity 'blocking' as 'BLOCKING: <title>' and counts it, including one marked `waived: inherited-baseline`. Its NEXT-ACTION line, printed below, says no fix is owed, so the builder is corrected on the same screen. critic_mode's verify-resolutions rule 1 counts it as actionable in the same way. Consider suffixing waived entries in the briefing render.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "A bare-string `failed_tests` fails the whole partial (the plan asked the boundary review to weigh this)", "recommendation": "With validate-partial as the net, and #961 limiting the cost to one reviewer, the strictness is affordable. Wrapping a lone non-empty string as a one-element list is unambiguous, though, and the dropped-id argument in the comment does not apply to it ('When a fail-closed validator guards a model-written field, tolerate the natural encoding variant'). That matters most where validate-partial cannot run, which is the finding above.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The 'interval names no base commit' refusal branch of `_inherited_baseline` has no test", "recommendation": "Reachable on a verify-resolutions dispatch whose prior review was of a dirty tree (`base_commit = prior head_commit`, null). It fails closed with a message. A one-case test would pin it beside the three tested 'base is not before the work' cases.", "files": ["tests/test_critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Small simplifications in the waiver path", "recommendation": "`_waive_inherited_failures` returns its list of waived fids, but its only caller checks the result only for None. The cache reads the waivers back from the store instead, so a bool or a sentinel would be enough. `_inherited_baseline` looks up the review fact by id again and re-scans its blocking findings, although its caller already holds that fact, so pass the fact in. The open-blocking arithmetic `blocking - len(waived)` lives in two places: `fact_to_cache_record`'s verdict and `next_action_line`. If either changes alone, they will disagree. None of this needs a fix round.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "coordinator.md's only change is a dropped trailing newline", "recommendation": "The diff to `plugin/skills/critic/coordinator.md` only removes the newline at the end of the file. The change record says the coordinator carries no remedy, so the file has no reason to be in the diff. Restoring the newline would keep it out of the bundle. No behaviour depends on it.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/coordinator.md"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "api-contract's 'exit 2 is a missing path' reads as 'the file does not exist'", "recommendation": "The code exits 2 only when the argument is absent. A path that names no existing file exits 1, with an 'unreadable' message. 'Missing argument' would say what the code does. This is prose, and nothing reads the exit code. Only a reader trying the verb by hand could be misled.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The not-an-ancestor refusal reads every non-zero `git merge-base --is-ancestor` as 'the work sits on the base branch'", "recommendation": "`_inherited_baseline` treats rc != 0 as 'not an ancestor', but git returns 1 for that and 128 for an error (an unknown object, a shallow clone). On an error the reason line misdiagnoses the cause, though it still fails closed and writes no waiver. Branch on rc == 1 and name a git failure separately if this line is ever made visible (see the SubagentStop finding).", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: covered all five oracle files, and only narration one revision short remains", "recommendation": "I rated all five `files_oracle` records against the three bars: api-contract.md, build-plan-review-round-cost.md, data-model.md, changes/review-round-cost.md and owner-rulings.md. Claims I checked against the code all hold: `origin` has one reader (`dispositions.resolution_detail_index`, by grep); the gate reads only the disposition (`coverage_algebra.resolution_index`); the merge-base and reviewed-tree conditions; the run fact's `failed_tests`. The owner ruling's 'each merged reading is develop's plus #1152's declared raises, within one token' re-adds correctly on all 6 payload routes and all 4 methodology files I summed. Three items clear no bar and want no edit. (a) The plan's Goals still place the 2026-11-11 ruling 'on `feature/gate-clarity`', which has since merged; the ruling is now in this branch's owner-rulings.md. (b) Chunk 01's description says `coordinator.md` carries the remedy sentence; the Critic round removed it (R-8), so the file's only net diff is a dropped trailing newline. (c) The api-contract's 'exit 2 is a missing path' means a missing argument; a nonexistent file exits 1. The change record's shipped promise about the not-waived line is the one record claim that clears a bar, and it folds into the SubagentStop finding.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-review-round-cost.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/changes/review-round-cost.md"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The bare-string `failed_tests` strictness is recorded and deliberate, against hook-surface's tolerance rule", "recommendation": "The plan records the choice to refuse a bare-string `failed_tests` and leaves it for the boundary review to weigh. The choice departs from 'When a fail-closed validator guards a model-written field, tolerate the natural encoding variant and hard-fail only on genuine ambiguity'. It is recorded with its reason (the issue's acceptance), and validate-partial plus the one-reviewer remedy now cap the cost of a refusal at one reviewer. On Goal 5 it is deliberate. Wrapping a bare string as a one-element list would also be safe, because an unmatched id only withholds a waiver. Whether that tolerance is worth having is a design call, not a gap in the record.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-10"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "review-round-cost", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunk": null, "base_reviewed": "c77a00b0449be8d45ec3046b0fe610bff63d2650", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/ar